A Wire parameter asks the caller to preserve the identity of one named local
binding. No special marker is written on the declaration or argument.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    read_name |value Wire of String| -> String:
        return value
    ;

    forward_name |value Wire of String| -> String:
        return read_name(value)
    ;

    replace_name |value ~String, replacement String|:
        value = replacement
    ;

    current_name ~= "Priya"
    label = read_name(current_name)
    current_name = "Rob"
    replace_name(~current_name, "Emmy")
]

The receiving parameter determines the use. Passing `current_name` to an
ordinary `String` parameter reads its current value. Passing it to another
compatible Wire parameter forwards the same binding identity. A Wire grants no
mutation permission: `replace_name` has an ordinary exclusive parameter, so
the call supplies `~current_name`. By contrast, `current_name ~= "Priya"`
declares a mutable binding and later `current_name = "Rob"` reassigns it. The
binding declaration and exclusive call argument have different jobs.

The identity belongs to this binding instance, not its current text. Reassigning
the mutable local keeps the same identity; a later function call or loop
iteration has its own local instance. A temporary expression, literal, field or
computed value cannot supply Wire identity directly.

`#=` does not declare a Wire or another runtime binding. A fully folded record
stored in a named constant is compile-time data, not a runtime type declaration:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    labels #= (
        first = "Priya",
        second = "Rob",
    )
]

Read the ordinary value contract in @../constants/#const-records (Const records)
and the full binding-identity rules in the detailed Wiring reference. The Wire
examples describe accepted future design and are unsupported until Phase 6.
